print('Hello, world!');
Certainly! Below is a JavaScript program that utilizes some advanced features such as Promises, async/await, and the Fetch API to make an HTTP request to a public API and manipulate the response:

```javascript
const fetchJsonData = async (url) => {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(url);
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP error! Status: ${response.status}`);
    return await response.json();
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Failed to fetch data:', error);
  }
};

const processData = (data) => {
  if (!Array.isArray(data)) throw new Error('Invalid data format');
  return data
    .map((item) => ({ title: item.title.toUpperCase(), id: item.id }))
    .filter((item) => item.id % 2 === 0);
};

const logResults = (data) => {
  console.group('Processed Data');
  data.forEach((item) => console.log(item));
  console.groupEnd();
};

(async () => {
  const url = 'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos';
  const rawData = await fetchJsonData(url);
  const processedData = processData(rawData);
  logResults(processedData);
})();
```

This code performs the following operations:
1. Uses the Fetch API to retrieve JSON data from a public API.
2. Utilizes async/await for handling asynchronous operations.
3. Processes the data by mapping and filtering items.
4. Logs the processed data to the console using console.group for organized output.